L["judas-kiss"] = """While Jesus was still speaking in the garden, a crowd arrived. It was led by Judas, one of the twelve. He had been a disciple for three years. He had heard the teaching and seen the miracles, and he had been trusted with the group's money. But he had agreed with the chief priests to hand Jesus over for thirty pieces of silver. That was the price of a slave in the law of Moses.

Judas had given the crowd a signal. The one I kiss is the man. Arrest him. A kiss on the cheek was a normal greeting between a student and a teacher. He had picked the sign of friendship to point out his friend.

Going at once to Jesus, Judas said, greetings, Rabbi! and kissed him. Jesus replied, friend, do what you came for. In Luke's account, Jesus asked him, Judas, are you betraying the Son of Man with a kiss? Even then, Jesus called him friend. He gave Judas a chance to turn back.

Then the men stepped forward, seized Jesus and arrested him. One of Jesus' companions, whom John names as Peter, reached for his sword and struck the servant of the high priest, cutting off his ear. Jesus said, no more of this! And he touched the man's ear and healed him. Put your sword back in its place, he said to him, for all who draw the sword will die by the sword. Do you think I cannot call on my Father, and he will at once put at my disposal more than twelve legions of angels? But how then would the Scriptures be fulfilled that say it must happen in this way?

Then Jesus said to the crowd, am I leading a rebellion, that you have come out with swords and clubs to capture me? Every day I sat in the temple courts teaching, and you did not arrest me. But this has all taken place that the writings of the prophets might be fulfilled. Then all the disciples deserted him and fled.

Why does this matter? Jesus was not captured. He gave himself up. He had the power to stop it and chose not to. He also showed love in the middle of betrayal, healing a wounded enemy and calling his betrayer friend. And everything happened according to what the prophets had written.

What happened after? Judas was filled with remorse when he saw that Jesus was condemned. He tried to return the money, but the priests wouldn't take it. He threw it down in the temple and left. His story ended in despair. The Bible doesn't hide how heavy that is. Peter also failed that night, but his story went on to forgiveness.

What does this mean for us today? Betrayal by someone close is among the deepest pains there is, and Jesus knows that pain. He also shows us how to respond, without revenge, and still reaching out. And it is a warning. Judas was close to Jesus and still betrayed him. Being near religious things is not the same as trusting him. We need to guard our hearts.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus went to the cross willingly, even when betrayed by a friend. And it shows a love that does not stop at betrayal."""

L["peter-denies"] = """After Jesus was arrested, they led him to the house of the high priest Caiaphas. Peter followed at a distance. John, another disciple, got him into the courtyard. It was a cold night, and the servants and officials stood around a fire they had made. Peter joined them and sat down to warm himself.

Earlier that night, Peter had been very confident. At the Last Supper, he had said, even if all fall away on account of you, I never will. Jesus had told him, truly I tell you, this very night, before the rooster crows, you will disown me three times. Peter insisted, even if I have to die with you, I will never disown you. All the other disciples said the same.

Now a servant girl saw him sitting there in the firelight. She looked closely at him and said, this man was with him. But he denied it. Woman, I don't know him. A little later someone else saw him and said, you also are one of them. Man, I am not, Peter replied. About an hour later, another one insisted, certainly this fellow was with him, for he is a Galilean. Peter replied, man, I don't know what you're talking about. Just as he was speaking, the rooster crowed.

The Lord turned and looked straight at Peter. Luke tells us this detail. Jesus was being led through the courtyard at that moment. Then Peter remembered the word the Lord had spoken to him. Before the rooster crows today, you will disown me three times. And he went outside and wept bitterly.

Why does this matter? Peter was not a coward by nature. He had stepped out of a boat onto the water and drawn a sword against a crowd. But in a moment of fear, he failed. The story is honest about how even the closest followers of Jesus can fall. Peter's confidence was the thing that failed him.

What happened after? After the resurrection, Jesus appeared to his disciples by the Sea of Galilee. By a charcoal fire, he asked Peter three times, do you love me? Peter, hurt, answered each time, Lord, you know all things. You know that I love you. Each time, Jesus said, feed my sheep. The three denials were matched by three affirmations, and Peter was restored. Fifty days later, he stood up in Jerusalem and preached boldly to thousands.

What does this mean for us today? We all have moments we are not proud of, times we said we would stand firm and didn't. Peter's story says that failure is not the end. Jesus had said in advance, I have prayed for you, Simon, that your faith may not fail. And when you have turned back, strengthen your brothers. Jesus knew Peter would fail, and he planned for his return. The tears of Peter were the start of a deeper faith.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus forgives and restores people who have failed him. And it gives hope to every follower who has fallen short."""
